Body:
**Workaround (until this is fixed in the SDK)**

For anyone hitting this, here's what we ended up doing in [@kud/ai-conventional-commit-cli](https://github.com/kud/ai-conventional-commit-cli):

Before calling `createOpencode()`, temporarily redirect `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` to a temp directory containing a minimal `opencode/config.json` with an empty MCP section. Since the SDK spawns `opencode serve` with `{ ...process.env }` at call time, the child process picks up the isolated config and starts with zero MCP servers.

```ts
import { mkdirSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';

// Create isolated config: <tmp>/aicc-<pid>/opencode/config.json
const isolatedDir = join(tmpdir(), `aicc-${process.pid}`, 'opencode');
mkdirSync(isolatedDir, { recursive: true });
writeFileSync(join(isolatedDir, 'config.json'), '{"mcp":{}}');

const originalXDG = process.env.XDG_CONFIG_HOME;
process.env.XDG_CONFIG_HOME = join(tmpdir(), `aicc-${process.pid}`);

try {
  opencode = await createOpencode({ signal, port });
} finally {
  if (originalXDG === undefined) delete process.env.XDG_CONFIG_HOME;
  else process.env.XDG_CONFIG_HOME = originalXDG;
}
```

This works on macOS and Linux where opencode respects `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`. It's a bit of a hack but eliminates the overhead of connecting/disconnecting all user MCP servers on every CLI invocation.
